{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BU7MaxWzgVTqUXnGBKeN7J","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted results for every \"planned\" experiment in the experiment_plan, under the \"median\" tier scenario they've defined. Let me identify the planned experiments:\n\n1. **gold-set** (id: gold-set) - Human-annotated 650 windows — GO/NO-GO gate, status: planned\n2. **f1-stag-analysis** (id: f1-stag-analysis) - F1_stag per-class breakdown, status: planned\n\nLet me also check if there are any other planned experiments I might have missed... Looking through the experiment_plan, only these two have `status: planned`.\n\n\nNow I'm working through the median tier definition to generate predicted results for both experiments. The median scenario shows structural approaches maintaining an advantage over probe methods, but with the gap narrowing significantly—from around 9 percentage points down to 3-8 points. I'm also factoring in that label bias becomes visible in this tier without fundamentally changing the conclusions, and I have specific performance ranges for each method across the gold set validation.\n\nFor the gold-set experiment specifically, I'm calculating midpoint values across those ranges: structural RF should hit around 0.84 macro F1, probe around 0.79, and the LLM judge around 0.71. This gives me the concrete predictions I need to build out the full experimental narrative.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the sub-experiments that would validate these predictions—starting with LF-human agreement analysis, then re-evaluating all methods against the gold labels, building a hybrid ensemble, doing detailed error analysis, and finally quantifying how much circular evaluation inflates the metrics.\n\nFor the F1_stag breakdown, I need to show how stagnation detection performs across different conditions and class distributions, which will be the key to understanding where the structural approach actually gains its advantage.\n\nLooking at the gold-set metrics more carefully: cmd_rep fires constantly on SWE-Agent but human annotators will disagree when repetition is actually exploratory—like running grep with different patterns. OB lags by about 7 steps, so there's a window where it triggers before true stagnation begins. file_diff is the most reliable since it's nearly concurrent with actual stagnation. This all points to around 80% agreement with most disagreements concentrated in cmd_rep-dominated cases.\n\nThe structural RF model achieves near-perfect performance on LF labels but will drop significantly on gold labels—roughly 15 percentage points—because it's essentially learned to reverse-engineer the LF rules, and when gold labels diverge from those rules, the structural features lose their predictive power.\n\nThe probe model shows less inflation than structural since it relies on semantic features that capture real stagnation patterns beyond just the LF heuristics. Where LF labels are wrong, the probe has some independent signal from semantic diversity that cmd_rep misses, so the performance gap narrows considerably.\n\nThe LLM judge performs worse on LF labels but actually improves on gold labels because its correct judgments were being penalized by the LF labels themselves.  It can detect semantic stagnation that the LFs miss, though it still lags behind structural due to its own systematic errors like context window limitations.\n\nThe hybrid structural+probe ensemble combines both the strong baseline patterns and complementary semantic signals, gaining a small boost over structural alone precisely where structural and LF labels fail.\n\nNow I'm working through the YAML configuration for the f1-stag-analysis, focusing on the median tier breakdown that shows where stagnation classification specifically degrades on gold labels.\n\nGenerating markdown tables... F1_prog across all methods on gold with per-class performance curves. Let me nail down these numbers precisely. I'm working through the median tier predictions and now computing the implied values to validate the inflation metrics—checking that structural RF shows about 15.5pp inflation on gold labels, the probe exhibits less circular bias at 11.5pp, and the LLM judge actually improves when moving from noisy LF labels to gold, while the structural-probe gap shrinks from 9pp to 5pp as expected.\n\nNow I'm estimating per-LF human agreement rates, where cmd_rep likely sits around 0.72 due to its high firing rate creating false stagnation labels that humans would dispute, file_diff reaches ~0.86 as the most objective signal, and OB lands near 0.78 with boundary disagreements from its lagging nature. For the hybrid ensemble, combining structural and probe methods should yield roughly 0.86 on gold.\n\nLooking at cross-agent structural performance, the gold labels show about 0.82 accuracy compared to 0.9674 on LF labels—a similar 15-point inflation gap driven by the same circularity issue. Now I'm breaking down the stagnation class specifically: the structural model's F1 for stagnation drops significantly from 0.9928 on LF to around 0.78 on gold, while progression stays higher at 0.89, and the probe approach reaches roughly 0.73 on stagnation F1.\n\nThe key insight is that cmd_rep's systematic over-firing creates a directional bias—more windows get reclassified from LF-stagnation to gold-progression than the reverse. This hits the stagnation class harder because the probe's correct stagnation predictions become false positives against the gold labels, while correct progression predictions become false negatives, and the imbalance favors stagnation losses.\n\nStructural features amplify this problem since they're essentially mirroring the LF behavior. Now I'm thinking through the breakdown dimensions for the f1-stagnation analysis—trajectory position, confidence levels, agent-specific patterns, LF firing signatures, and whether cases resolved or remained unresolved. I have enough to structure the response now, and I'm also mapping out what the gold-set experiment would reveal: annotation statistics and inter-annotator agreement, how well LF and gold align overall and per-function, how each method performs when re-evaluated on gold labels, the quantified inflation from circular evaluation, results from combining structural and probe approaches, and what patterns emerge in the gold annotations themselves.\n\nFor the f1-stagnation analysis specifically, I need per-class breakdowns across methods on gold, F1 scores stratified by trajectory phase and LF confidence, outcome-based comparisons, and the precision-recall tradeoff in detecting stagnation. The narrative should align with the median tier direction the user described. I'm finalizing the YAML structure now with detailed rationales, and I'm also clarifying the two core experiments: the gold-set with 650 human-annotated windows as P0, and the f1-stagnation analysis. Now I'm thinking through the specific metrics this analysis would surface—on gold labels, F1 stagnation scores should be consistently lower across all methods compared to the labeling function results, with the stagnation class being where the labeling function bias is most pronounced due to command repetition false positives. The position-based and confidence-based breakdowns would reveal that middle segments and low-confidence windows show the largest divergence between gold and labeling function performance, while the probe method should recover some ground on stagnation detection specifically because it has semantic understanding for edge cases.\n\nWriting the YAML output...\n\nFor the gold-set experiment with 650 windows sampled from the full dataset, I'm estimating the stratification across confidence levels—likely around 50 from the limited conf3 pool, 300 from conf2, and 300 from conf1—which means the overall LF-human agreement would be a weighted average across these tiers, ranging from ~95% on high-confidence cases down to ~65% on ambiguous ones, giving an overall agreement around 76%. I'm reconsidering the composition of the gold set with a stratified approach—pulling more from the high-confidence examples for validation, a solid chunk from the middle tier, and the bulk from lower-confidence cases where disambiguation matters most. Running through the weighted agreement calculation with these proportions gives me around 0.79 overall, which feels reasonable for the LF-human baseline. Now I need to think through the methodology.\n\nThe crucial part is understanding how models trained on LF labels will diverge when evaluated against gold. On the ~79% of cases where they align, performance should track closely to what we see on LF labels. But on the ~21% where they disagree, models will systematically fail because they've learned the LF pattern rather than the true signal. The damage isn't uniform though—structural RF gets hit hardest since it's essentially reverse-engineering the LF rules and will almost always follow them on disagreement cases, while the probe has some independent semantic grounding that might accidentally align with gold, and the LLM judge actually benefits since it was never constrained by LF training in the first place.\n\nNow I'm working through the actual numbers. If I estimate gold accuracy on disagreement windows at roughly 20% for structural RF, 35% for the probe, and 60% for the LLM judge, I can compute expected gold macro F1 scores. The structural RF should land around 0.83-0.84, the probe around 0.79, and the LLM judge lower at 0.71. That gives me a 5 percentage point gap between structural RF and probe on gold, which is interesting because it's smaller than the circular evaluation inflation we're seeing with structural RF on LF labels.\n\nFor the hybrid approach combining structural and probe signals, I'd expect maybe a 2 percentage point improvement over structural alone since the probe has some independent signal on the disagreement windows. Cross-agent performance on gold should stay consistent with the in-domain inflation pattern, landing around 0.82.\n\nNow I'm looking at the per-LF agreement patterns, starting with cmd_rep—it fires at 97.4% on SWE-Agent when confidence is at least 2, but humans would likely disagree in cases where the agent is productively iterating through different files with repeated commands.\n\nfile_diff is the most objective signal since it's binary—either files changed or they didn't—giving it the highest agreement around 0.88. The outcome-based signal is more subjective since it depends on whether annotators agree with the backward propagation logic, landing around 0.77 agreement.\n\nThe main source of disagreement on gold is cmd_rep falsely flagging stagnation when humans see progress, accounting for about 60% of the LF-gold mismatches. file_diff turns out to be the most reliable LF overall, while the outcome-based signal shows moderate disagreement in boundary windows near stagnation onset.\n\nI have enough detail now to structure the final YAML. For the f1-stag-analysis section, I need to think through what specific metrics to predict: overall f1_stag across methods, broken down by trajectory position, original LF confidence, and LF agreement patterns, plus the improvement from the hybrid approach. On gold labels, I'll need the structural RF f1 baseline and how it compares across these dimensions.\n\nNow I'm looking at the performance metrics across different methods on gold labels versus the LF predictions. The structural RF shows significant drops—f1_stag falls from 0.99 to around 0.78, and f1_prog drops from 0.997 to 0.89. The probe model also drops substantially. The LLM judge is interesting because it actually improves slightly on gold, moving from around 0.66 to 0.71 macro f1, which suggests it generalizes better than the other methods.\n\nFor the position-based analysis, I'm seeing that stagnation detection is hardest in the